 by  saunder lock who was the creative  director for csmarzen who  um then took all of the textiles that we  created and turn them into garments and  i'll just show you  a little bit here you can get a sense of  the um  the garments in motion in this slow  mo pick and i think i'm at the end of my  time  so i want to make sure i saw that there  were some things coming up in the chat  and i'm happy to start to answer those  questions  um i know that i know some of you  probably have to  to take off um but maybe i'll stop  sharing my screen and that way i can  start to answer some of these questions  and if others have questions please feel  free to  to speak up um wonderful well  an absolutely fascinating lecture denise  you know so many of us  work with plants in various ways but  we don't usually work with them in in  these applications so  it really is uh expanding for us  i see that there's a number of questions  that have to do with  mordants yes um so you were asking what  is the toxicity and health risks  so the toxicity and health risks will  depend on the mordant that's used  aluminum acetate and aluminum sulfate  are considered low risk  but something like a chrome or tin right  very very dangerous so  and again if you inhale anything it's  going to be dangerous so even if we call  aluminum acetate low risk if you're not  wearing a mask  and you're dumping it into a bucket and  the cloud is coming up and you breathe  in that's that's going to be very  bad for you oh gutta gutta is um  the question you would also ask the  question about what is gutta um  this is uh it's actually not natural  uh usually it's a petroleum byproduct it  feels really  um gummy actually and it's used in silk  painting to draw those white lines  um which creates a resist  to get it out you typically have to take  your textile to a dry cleaner  and like a not a non-toxic dry like the  toxic dry cleaner so  it's not an ideal um use allison asked  do we know what our ancestors used for  mordants  um so that's a great question and it  will depend on what part of the world  your ancestors were from  um in this region we we would imagine  that people were most likely using um  for the tannic acid for example  would have come from uh oh  it's it's eluding me now um  what is the one with the red cones that  we see everywhere  um um ruth to find a staghorn sumac  thank you um so we know sumac was used  to to get a lot of  tannic acid into the bath um but that  that's a really good question and  would require i think a lot a lot more  research into the specific  regions um uh okay  so uh when you get questions from uh  marcia what morden do you use with  eucalyptus  in eco printing so with eucalyptus  i i basically the mornings i use tend to  be whatever the  is the ideal mordant for the fiber type  so if i'm dying silk with eucalyptus i  would use  um i would use aluminum sulfate  but that said another technique that um  blossom schmidt one of my  my former students she has been  exploring  soaking different uh plants  leaves etc in uh ferrous sulfate and  iron  and like water that's that's got some  iron in it and then  putting those plants onto textiles and  then eco printing and she's gotten some  really amazing results  using iron um on the actual  uh um plant leaves and and whatnot  um gregory asked what is the potential  for local farmers to grow and sell dye  plants and or plant-based dyes is there  enough of a market  are large clothing companies interested  in natural dyes  the answer is yes there is a growing  market  our alumni some of you may know sarah  bellows  uh started a company called stoney creek  colors in tennessee  where she's working with um former  tobacco farmers or tobacco farmers to  convert their fields to indigo  she has a die house there in tennessee  um and so  there is a growing market we've seen  patagonia  all birds eileen fisher some of these  bigger large  you know fashion brands and smaller  fashion brands as well  are starting to experiment with natural  dyes  and so i think that there is a growing  market  um that said the bottleneck right now  is production right so we don't have die  houses  that are used to working with these  kinds of dyes either here  and abroad um so i  bring my students to india usually every  other year and we visited some natural  dye facilities but still relatively  small  scale i'm going to interrupt for just a  moment  and say that unfortunately i have to go  off and teach a class  but i invite all of you who don't have  immediate  commitments to stay with denise because  i see that there's  a number of additional questions and  again denise  to me this is an example of what is so  wonderful to  uh being at cornell is that we have  colleagues who are doing  such fascinating things so thank you so  much  thank you so much don for the invitation  and have a great class  thank you um and i'm happy to stay on  and talk to people and try to get  through  um the rest of these questions uh  do i have any favorite natural dye books  um  there has there has there are a lot of  really good books and i'd be happy to  put together a list  um but i i say  personally i've done most of my  education reading here and there but  really like trial and error  um on my own and just experimenting has  been  has been really my my method  but i i do have a library of books i'd  be happy to share some of the  titles with you do you know any  fermentation techniques to  increase dye color or color fastness  and how can this be used to enhance  results that is a great question ruby um  that is not something that i have  personally experimented with so i  i can't really say uh but i  know i mean uh indigo is a kind of  fermentation process  we know that with that particular dye  that sort of it needs to happen but  i would be curious to to know what  what others have been working with and  there are other folks doing that kind of  work  what if we have something that we think  might be a good die for example  petals on some ornamental with an  interesting color how can we test the  dye  um is there any rapid tests that let you  know whether something is good or not i  would say  the probably the easiest test to do at  home we have a testing lab in our  department but at home you could morton  your fabric dye it with  the material and then um you can do your  own washing test you could run it  through the washing machine  a few times or another thing that you  can do is like  uh stick it like with a piece of tape  stick it to a  south facing window uh that has gets a  lot of light  and then c you know put one swatch away  and  you know close it up in a dark place  tape a swatch to a bright window  and then in two weeks time see if  there's much of a difference or  you might see a huge difference in a  day's time  um so that will help you to understand  at home at least you know without any  fancy testing equipment  you can do your own lightness uh testing  and wash testing at home  um and i think uh oh ruby i will  yeah why don't you email me ruby and i  will put you in touch with some folks  at kansas state university they have an  amazing natural dye lab  and there are um when i visited uh  the agricultural uh university in tel  uh telangana uh they also have a natural  dye production facility where they're  doing  lots and lots of research so i'd be  happy to share that with you  um and thank you everyone for coming  today it was really nice  to be able to share this work and talk  with all of you  i had one last question oh yeah sure so  for  your uh dying with hammering that you  showed at the end  that was sent to new york city what kind  of like washing is done with that and  does it have to be dry cleaned and how  long would those colors fade  yeah so that's a really good question um  again the issue is around scalability so  basically what they ended up doing uh to  be honest  is they took the designs that we did and  then they digitized them  and they digitally printed them so what  ended up ultimately happening for like  the production  uh aspect of it because obviously like  hammering  by hand all of you know there could be a  way to  scale up the production of textiles like  that but  in their case they digitize them and  then digitally print for mass production  but for the fashion show  they use the original textiles um  the the trouble with greens in  particular those leaves so we used sage  we used um  japanese indigo leaves they look green  for a while  but it's not color fast and as that  chlorophyll starts it to shift it  and go away essentially you end up with  more of a brown over time so my guess is  in a year  everything that we saw was green will be  brown and that was  they understood that as part of the  process and  and ultimately ended up digitizing the  textiles to print them  that way which i you know is a little  bit disappointing  but you know they're trying to at least  you know experiment with these  techniques and hopefully we might see  ways of manufacturing at a larger scale  in the future  yeah thank you jonah any other  final questions  okay well thank you all it was nice to  meet you and thanks  for listening have a great day  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


